  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview: \n\nThe upper ridge axis over the CONUS Central Plains will continue\nto propagate eastward with strong southerly flow over Montana\ntransitioning to Southwesterly flow over the next 12 to 24 hours.\nWith support from a broad negatively tilted trough extending from\nthe PNW through Western CONUS, a weak upper-level disturbance \nwill perturbate through the flow bringing generally light showery\nprecipitation over Southwest MT this morning. Due to surface\nwarming and ABL mixing, WBZ heights will stay around 3000 FT so \nsnow will be containted to the mountain ranges. There is a stable \nlayer over the Northern regions of the state with weak easterly \nflow keeping low-level fog producing moisture trapped along the \nMilk River Valley until this afternoon when diurnal boundary layer\nmixing occurs. \n\nFor Sunday, warm and dry conditions will persist with\nSouthwesterly flow aloft maintaining warm air advection to keep\ntemperatures in the 50s and 60s. With the approaching Pacific \ntrough, pressure gradient winds will increase over Southwest MT \nwith gusts up to 40 mph expected within the valley downslope \nregions Sunday morning into the afternoon. \n\nBeginning Monday night, the Pacific system will kick a strong \nupper-level shortwave trough out over western CONUS bringing deep\nascending moisture and instability over Montana into Wednesday and\nfueling cyclogenesis over the Yellowstone area. This low pressure\nsystem will deepen and move eastward across the Continental Divide\npulling colder Canadian air south into the region and creating\nwidespread snow across the CWA by Tuesday night. Moisture laden \nSouthwesterly flow aloft with cold northerly advection will \nprovide healthy snow growth zone profiles over much of the state.\nMost likely placement for a weak deformation zone to develop will\nbe Northwest of the low over Western and Central MT. - Akins\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios: \n\nThe persistence of fog this morning is high for the Havre and\nMilk River Valley areas however current REFS guidance is about\n40% probability for the fog extending westward to Shelby and Cut\nBank. For the upcoming winter event Tuesday, ensembles are \nsignalling a potential weak deformation zone for moderate snowfall\nsetting up approximately over Helena and Great Falls. ECMWF and \nGFS show similar mesoscale band placement within the broader \npattern however intensity, exact location, and duration still \ndiverge. Most snowfall estimates for lower elevation areas are 1-4\ninches over the Monday night to Wednesday timeframe. Next run \nwill bring event time within most short- edium range ensemble \nmembers for higher fidelity scenario creation. - Akins",
